A||||6||Jim Baumann||Overheating||||5
Z||000000||Jim Baumann||10-14-1999||08:09 PM||||Just bought my first Fiero 3 months ago, a 86 with a v6 4spd. I knew they had some overheating problems but this baffels me.  First the heatercore started leaking.  I took a hose and bypassed it.  A week later the water pump went out, water started coming out that little check hole in the body of the pump so I replaced it.  Then several days after that the radiator started leaking.  Got that fixed(needed a new side tank, the seal between the plastic tank and aluminum core gave out) and reinstalled it.  I did all the burping and stuff mentioned in the manuels as well as this and other forums on how to get air out of the system but now when I drive and get the car warmed up the water goes into the recovery bottle and overflows at the top.  When the engine cools the water gets sucked back in but because of the overflow the system is not "full" any more.  After several trips I have to refill the system.  The cooling fan does come on when necessary.  Any Ideas?  Do I need to replace the heatercore for it to work right?  I used to have a 86 6000 with a v6 and had that heater core bypassed.  Still have a 195 degree thermostat in, would a lower one be better?  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||RICK||10-14-1999||08:30 PM||||Somebody correct me if I’m wrong, but, the front radiator cap should be spring loaded which allows for expansion if the cooling system builds up to much pressure caused from the coolant expanding.  If this spring is week or broke wouldn’t this allow for the coolant to flow freely into the overflow when the coolant started to get warm?||||reg||5
Z||000003||fiero56||10-14-1999||10:02 PM||||I had this problem with my Formula after I bought it.  Turned out the cap had no spring pressure left.  After I replaced I had no troubles.  But since I do most of my driving in the city, I put in a 180 degree thermostat to help keep the engine a little cooler, and it helps.  My resoviour(sp?) only overflowed after I parked the car for about 5 minutes, then it would dump nearly a gallon or two of coolant.  I would replace the cap first, it's pretty inexpensive to start out with.  By the way, if  you get a Stant cap, their book is wrong, at least at the shop near me.  You want a 16lb release UNVENTED, the book recommeds vented.  Don't get it, unless you want a nice surprise.||||reg||1
Z||000004||g2||10-15-1999||02:47 AM||||I had a similar problem a couple of years ago. Turned out the culprit was a bad replacement water pump.  Some of the aftermarket pumps use a plastic impellor, the problem is that in time the impellor gets a little loose, you end up with no or very little circulation and the resulting steam boilover forces most of the coolant out of the radiator and overflow tank. To make matters worse the problem can be intermittent when it first starts to occur.<BR>I'd look at all of the easier cures first but  the symptoms were exactly what you described.<BR>If you decide to get a new pump, make sure it  uses a metal impellor on a metal shaft. (By the way, I discovered this problem after talking to a friend who had the same problem on his Cadillac when he had replaced his pump with an OEM pump with a plastic impellor.)||||reg||12
Z||000005||Bill Osmer||10-15-1999||10:23 AM||||I just got my '86 GT about 9 weeks ago, and went through the same troubleshooting efforts. I tried an interesting trick on the coolant lines: insert a 6 inch length of CLEAR 1 1/2" Tygon tubing (available from Lowe's) along with an appropriate galvanized nipple between the upper hose (going into the top of the radiator). This will provide you with a "viewport", which will let you see if any coolant is flowing. This will provide absolute feedback when the water pump (which was the source of my overheating prblems)works. NOTE: I also removed the radiator and flushed it inside and out - very dirty inside and out).||||reg||3
Z||000006||fieroshop||10-15-1999||11:26 AM||||Several things that will cause an engine to overheat are.<BR>1. Bad water pump.<BR>2. Bad radiator.<BR>3. dirty radiator.<BR>4 plugged cooling lines.<BR>5. Bad radiator cap also check and make sure both caps are on tight.<BR>6. Blown head gasket and a compression test will help reveal the problem.<BR>7. Cracked head or cracked block.<BR>Sorry but I am not trying to scare you just the things you need to lokk at. Also when ever I replace water pumps I do not use new water pumps since they all seem to have plastic impellers. You need to make sure that you use water pumps that have steel impellers in them.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Carrolles||10-16-1999||11:02 PM||||Take a look under the car at the steel cooling pipes that run from front to back under each rocker panel to see that neither is crushed.  It would be  easy to crush one if the car is jacked up incorrectly from either side.||||reg||1
